From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: rumisle <why-excuse-quail@duck.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 18:32:31 +0800
Subject: web/history-sync: delta resync, a history cache, and restoring far
 back in one request

The client syncs a session with message-sync: a reconnect costs the changes, not the whole loaded
transcript again. The rules live in client/solid/message-sync.ts (pure, tested directly):
mergeSync keeps held copies by identity, merges changed ones into their rows, drops what the
answer leaves out and keeps what arrived by event while the request was out; cacheChange says
what the cache should hold. A sync and a page load never overlap (one lock per session).

Streamed deltas apply at their offset: duplicates are dropped, a gap refetches the message with
deltas held meanwhile, block starts already read are not added twice.

The web app keeps each session's newest messages in IndexedDB, so a reload shows the transcript
and the saved place at once. Every record carries the server's update time and the next sync
checks it, so another tab or a failed write costs a refetch, never wrong data (per app build,
15 sessions, 3000 messages each). Older pages reach it with a quiet sync 0.8 s after loading.
loadUntil brings a place far back in with one request. Transcript reads go ahead of other
queued requests.

Restoring at first paint exposed races in the restore. A saved place now decides where the
timeline opens (the hash scroll no longer pins the bottom meanwhile). The restore writes the
offset itself and follows the row by key until it holds still, instead of TanStack's
scrollToIndex, which kept steering to an index that a prepended page had turned into another
row. A place also keeps the next rows on screen: the top row's key can change with what is
loaded around it (a group of steps cut at a page boundary groups differently once complete), and
falling back to the turn's start reopened hundreds of pixels off.

Ocelot-Patch: web/history-sync

